Subject: [PATCH] ntfs: allow colon (':') in file names with windows_names

The windows_names mount option makes ntfs_check_bad_char() reject ':'
when creating, renaming or linking files. udisks mounts ntfs with
windows_names by default, so file managers' mounts can't hold a Wine
or Proton prefix: Wine needs the dosdevices/c: and z: links.

A colon is valid in an on-disk NTFS name. Windows reads "a:b" as
file "a", stream "b", so such names can't be opened from Windows,
but on the Linux side they work as long as streams_interface=windows
is not used.

Drop the 0x003A comparison. windows_names still rejects the other
reserved characters, reserved device names and trailing space/dot.
---
diff --git a/fs/ntfs/namei.c b/fs/ntfs/namei.c
index b46fe44b6..7b2a3079c 100644
--- a/fs/ntfs/namei.c
+++ b/fs/ntfs/namei.c
@@ -51,7 +51,7 @@ static inline int ntfs_check_bad_char(const __le16 *wc, unsigned int wc_len)
 
 		if (c < 0x0020 ||
 		    c == 0x0022 || c == 0x002A || c == 0x002F ||
-		    c == 0x003A || c == 0x003C || c == 0x003E ||
+		    c == 0x003C || c == 0x003E ||
 		    c == 0x003F || c == 0x005C || c == 0x007C)
 			return -EINVAL;
 	}
